{
  name: "keymgr_dpe"
  import_testplans: ["hw/dv/tools/dvsim/testplans/csr_testplan.hjson",
                     "hw/dv/tools/dvsim/testplans/intr_test_testplan.hjson",
                     "hw/dv/tools/dvsim/testplans/alert_test_testplan.hjson",
                     "hw/dv/tools/dvsim/testplans/tl_device_access_types_testplan.hjson",
                     "hw/dv/tools/dvsim/testplans/shadow_reg_errors_testplan.hjson",
                     "hw/dv/tools/dvsim/testplans/sec_cm_count_testplan.hjson",
                     "hw/dv/tools/dvsim/testplans/sec_cm_fsm_testplan.hjson",
                     "hw/dv/tools/dvsim/testplans/stress_all_with_reset_testplan.hjson",
                     "keymgr_dpe_sec_cm_testplan.hjson"]
  testpoints: [
    {
      name: smoke
      desc: '''
            Smoke test exercises the base commands implemented in keymgr_dpe:
            - Advance
            - Versioned key generation (namely: Generate HW output and Generate SW output)
            - Erase
            - Disable

            Two points:
            - Also, exportable policy bit is not implemented yet so setting / unsetting creates no
            effect.
            - Randomly set kmac_en_masking input in keymgr_dpe at the start of the test.

            Stimulus:
            - Randomly select a destination slot x and issue an advance operation (which should
              latch the root key into slot x). Update the SLOT_POLICY register field with the
              default root key policy i.e. allow_keygen=0, retain_parent=0, exportable=0, and
              allow_child=1.
            - Request another advance operation for the same slot (due to retain_parent=0 of the
              root key slot). Set allow_child and allow_keygen policy bits to true. Randomly set
              retain_parent field. Use random values for HW / SW bindings.
            - If retain_parent policy of the previously advanced slot is true, then issue an erase
              command on a random selection.
            - To generate version keys for HW sideload ports, issue Generate HW Output command to a
              random dest_sel inside {AES, HMAC, KMAC, OTBN).
            - To generate version keys for SW CSRs, issue Generate SW Output command. The results
              are going to be loaded in SW_SHARE0_OUTPUT and SW_SHARE1_OUTPUT.
            - Repeat the above steps a couple of times with randomly picking a valid parent slot. If
              retain_parent policy bit of the parent slot is true then randomly choose a different
              invalid child slot. Otherwise derive the secrets to the parent slot.
            - Issue the disable command at the end which will wipe all the slots.
            - Wipe all the HW sideload key ports and SW_SHARE*_OUTPUT register.

            Checks:
            - Check INTR_STATE.op_done field to check the completion of the operation
            - Check OP_STATUS.status field to see if the operation was successful.
            - Check DPE context in METADATA_HIGH register.
            - Before issuing disable operation, check that the METADATA_* register fields for slots
              greater than equal to NumInstHwSlot are read back to 0.
            - Check the key stored in a slot for correctness.
            - Check that the HW sideload key slots and SW_SHARE*_OUTPUT registers are updated with
              the correct keys.
            '''

      stage: V1
      tests: ["keymgr_dpe_smoke"]
    }
    {
      name: load_key_command
      desc: '''
            Similar to smoke test but it also issues load root key and load root secondary key
            commands

            Stimulus:
            - Randomly select a destination slot x and issue an advance operation (which should
              latch the root key into slot x). Update the SLOT_POLICY register field with the
              default root key policy i.e. allow_keygen=0, retain_parent=0, exportable=0, and
              allow_child=1.
            - Request another advance operation for the same slot (due to retain_parent=0 of the
              root key slot). Set allow_child and allow_keygen policy bits to true. Randomly set
              retain_parent field. Use random values for HW / SW bindings.
            - If retain_parent policy of the previously advanced slot is true, then issue an erase
              command on a random selection.
            - To generate version keys for HW sideload ports, issue Generate HW Output command to a
              random dest_sel inside {AES, HMAC, KMAC, OTBN).
            - To generate version keys for SW CSRs, issue Generate SW Output command. The results
              are going to be loaded in SW_SHARE0_OUTPUT and SW_SHARE1_OUTPUT.
            - On a random selection, load either of the root key or secondary root key and set the
              slot DPE context with default root policy fields.
            - Repeat the steps 2-6 couple of times with randomly picking a valid parent slot. If
              retain_parent policy bit of the parent slot is true then randomly choose a different
              invalid slot. Otherwise derive the secrets to the parent slot.
            - Issue the disable command at the end which will wipe all the slots.
            - Wipe all the HW sideload key ports and SW_SHARE*_OUTPUT register.

            Checks:
            Same as smoke test.
            '''

      stage: V1
      tests: ["keymgr_dpe_load_key"]
    }
    {
      name: random_commands
      desc: '''
            Issue random commands to keymgr_dpe.

            Stimulus:
            - Send random operations to keymgr_dpe in random order in available state.
            - To request LOAD_*_KEY commands multiple times within the test, avoid setting the
              corresponding root key's LOAD_*_KEY_LOCK registers.

            Checks:
            Similar to smoke test.
            '''

      stage: V1
      tests: ["keymgr_dpe_rand_cmds"]
    }
    {
      name: csr_testing
      desc: '''
            Checks that keymgr_dpe performs the operations correctly based on the contents of a
            particular CSR.

            Stimulus:
            - Randomly set the fields in SIDELOAD_CLEAR, ENFFORCE_SW_BINDING,
              RESEED_INTERVAL_SHADOWED, and  LOAD_*_LOCK registers.
            - Issue regular operations via keymgr_dpe_rand_cmds test.

            Checks:
            - Check that the sideload ports are continuously cleared after the GENERATE_HW_OUTPUT
              operation based on SIDELOAD_CLEAR.val field.
            - Check that sideload ports cleared with entropy.
            - Check that the HW bindings are suppressed when ENFORCE_SW_BINDING.enforce field is
              set.
            - Check that the LOAD_*_KEY operation is rejected when the corresponding
              LOAD_*_LOCK.lock field is set. In this scenario, check ERR_CODE.invalid_op field is
              set.
            - Check that a reseed is requested after RESEED_INTERVAL_SHADOWED.val counter hits in
              keymgr_dpe.

      stage: V2
      tests: ["keymgr_dpe_csr_testing"]
    }
    {
      name: regwen
      desc: '''
            Verify that the registers gated by *regwen.EN are correctly passing through or ignoring
            their accesses based on their respective *regwen.EN register field.

            Stimulus:
            - Write to *regwen register to either enable and disable the writes to their respective
              gated registers.
            - After that, write to a register which is gated by *regwen.
            - Meanwhile during an operation, access a register that cannot be modified during an
              operation.

            Checks:
            - Expect to see a register either updated its contents or ignored the access based on
              its respective *regwen.EN.
            - Expect some related registers are non-modifiable during an operation.
            '''

      stage: V2
      tests: ["keymgr_dpe_regwen"]
    }
    {
      name: lc_disable
      desc: '''
            Inject disable through life cycle control at random durations while running the
            keymgr_dpe_rand_cmds test in parallel.

            Checks:
            - Expect to see DEBUG.inactive_lc_en in case of LC disable.
            - Expect state to be INVALID in WORKING_STATE.state field.
            - Expect to see all the HW sideload ports, SW keys in SW_SHARE*_OUTPUT registers and all
              the slots wiped in an INVALID state.
            '''

      stage: V2
      tests: ["keymgr_dpe_rand_disable"]
    }
    {
      name: stress_all
      desc: '''
            - Combine the non-error sequences (smoke, load_key_command, and random_commands) into a
              single test that runs them sequentially for multiple iterations.
            '''

      stage: V2
      tests: ["keymgr_dpe_stress_all"]
    }
    {
      name: invalid_hw_bindings
      desc: '''
            Issue all-ones or all-zeros as HW binding and check the ERR_CODE and FAULT_STATUS
            registers.

            Stimulus:
            - Once the keymgr_dpe comes into an available state, inject all-ones, all-zeros, or
              combination of them as HW bindings for key derivation function.

            Checks:
            - Check INTR_STATE.op_done field to check the completion of the operation.
            - Expect DEBUG register fields to trigger on invalid HW bindings.
            - Expect to see ERR_CODE.invalid_kmac_input.
            - Expect the slot to be invalid after the operation.
            - For valid HW bindings, check OP_STATUS.status field to see if the operation was
              successful.
            '''

      stage: V2
      tests: ["keymgr_dpe_invalid_hw_msgs"]
    }
    {
      name: invalid_sw_input
      desc: '''
            Perform commands with invalid SW inputs.

            Stimulus:
            - Randomize the contents that are required in an operation to bad values.
              Examples are:
              - Generating version key when KEY_VERSION > MAX_KEY_VERSION.
              - Derive a non-existing slot such that
                CONTROL_SHADOWED.slot_src_sel / CONTROL_SHADOWED.slot_dst_sel >= NumInstHwSlot.
              - Send an undefined command.
              - Write CONTROL_SHADOWED.dest_sel with undefined enumerated value.

            Checks:
            - Expect to see ERR_CODE.invalid_op.
            '''

      stage: V2
      tests: ["keymgr_dpe_sw_invalid_input"]
    }
    {
      name: invalid_slot_operations
      desc: '''
            Derive a secret from an empty/invalid slot to an occupied/valid slot.

            Stimulus:
            This list of stimuli includes:
            - Request an advance operation from an invalid slot or to a valid slot or both.
            - Request a version key generation operation from an invalid slot or to a valid slot or
              both.
            - Request an erase operation for an invalid slot.

            Checks:
            - Check ERR_CODE.invalid_op field is set as an evidence of triggering this testpoint.
            - Verify the METADATA registers are unchanged.
            '''

      stage: V2
      tests: ["keymgr_dpe_invalid_slot_ops"]
    }
    {
      name: invalid_parent_policy_operations
      desc: '''
            The parent policy of a DPE context determines how the operation on the child slot would
            be carried out. Whether a parent slot can derive secrets, generate a version key, derive
            a secret to its own slot or a different slot, etc depends on the parent's key_policy
            bits. Note that exportable policy bit is not implemented so setting / unsetting that
            creates no effect.

            Stimulus:
            The list of stimuli includes:
            - Request an advance operation from a slot having policy bit allow_child=false.
            - Request a versioned key generation operation from a slot having policy bit
              allow_keygen=false.
            - Request an advance operation for a different child slot with parent policy bit
              retain_parent=false
            - Request an advance operation for the same slot with parent policy bit
              retain_parent=true.

            Checks:
            - Check ERR_CODE.invalid_op field is set as an evidence of triggering this testpoint.
            - Verify the METADATA registers are unchanged.
            - Verify that the sideload ports and SW_SHARE*_OUTPUT registers are unchanged.
            '''

      stage: V2
      tests: ["keymgr_dpe_invalid_parent_policy_ops"]
    }
    {
      name: load_key_without_default_policy
      desc: '''
            Perform the loading operation of root / secondary root key (either in reset or via load
            commands) without the default UDS policy bits. The slot policy should not be updated by
            the non-default values written by SW.

            Stimulus:
            - Write the SLOT_POLICY register with some non-default slot policy.

            Checks:
            - Expect the slot to be valid by reading METADATA_HIGH.valid field.
            - Expect the METADATA_HIGH slot policy fields to return default values.
            '''

      stage: V2
      tests: ["keymgr_dpe_invalid_load_key_op"]
    }
    {
      name: load_key_command_in_valid_slot
      desc: '''
            Attempt to reload the root or secondary root key into a valid hardware slot.

            Stimulus:
            - Send load root / secondary-root key command and select valid slot as destination.

            Checks:
            - Check ERR_CODE.invalid_op field is set as an evidence of triggering this testpoint.
            - Verify the METADATA registers are unchanged.
            '''

      stage: V2
      tests: ["keymgr_dpe_invalid_load_key_op"]
    }
    {
      name: lock_loading_key_command
      desc: '''
            Attempt to load the root or secondary root key when their respective lock registers
            (LOAD_ROOT_KEY_LOCK and LOAD_SECONDARY_ROOT_KEY_LOCK) are set.

            Stimulus:
            - Set the lock register, then attempt to issue the load command.

            Checks:
            - Check ERR_CODE.invalid_op field is set as an evidence of triggering this testpoint.
            - Verify the METADATA registers are unchanged.
            '''

      stage: V2
      tests: ["keymgr_dpe_invalid_load_key_op"]
    }
    {
      name: invalid_root_key
      desc: '''
            Verify keymgr_dpe behavior when either of the root keys contains all-zeros / all-ones or
            driving their METADATA_HIGH.valid = 0 in the HW after loading it. This should cover both
            scenarios of latching the root key when WORKING_STATE.state = RESET or reloading root /
            secondary root key via their respective commands in WORKING_STATE.state=AVAILABLE state.

            Stimulus:
            The stimuli includes:
            - Driving all-zeros / all_ones to either of the root keys during the latching cycle or
              between a reloading command.
            - Invalidate either of the root key's in HW so that their METADATA_HIGH.valid field
              reads back as zero.

            Checks:
            - Expect to see DEBUG.invalid_root_key or DEBUG.invalid_secondary_root_key fields.
            - Expect to see METADATA_HIGH.valid=0.
            '''

      stage: V2
      tests: ["keymgr_dpe_invalid_load_key_op"]
    }
    {
      name: kmac_error_response
      desc: '''
            Verify keymgr_dpe behavior when an error response is received from KMAC after
            initiating a key derivation transaction.

            Stimulus:
            - Assert the KMAC error signal while the KMAC output valid signal is high.
            - Alternatively, drive all-zeros or all-ones as the KMAC digest output to trigger
              the output validity check.

            Checks:
            - Expect to see FAULT_STATUS.kmac_op field set.
            '''

      stage: V2
      tests: ["keymgr_dpe_kmac_err_rsp"]
    }
    {
      name: debug_reg
      desc: '''
            Perform operations that can trigger fields in the DEBUG register.

            Stimulus:
            Stimulus could be anything that can set fields in the DEBUG register. Few examples are
            listed below:
            - Invalidate either of the root key's in HW so that their METADATA_HIGH.valid field
              reads back as zero.
            - Write invalid content in the root keys.
            - Disable LC control that will deactivate the keymgr_dpe block.
            - Send invalid HW bindings mentioned in "invalid_hw_sw_bindings" testpoint.

            Checks:
            - Check corresponding DEBUG register fields.
            - Check WORKING_STATE.state field in case of INVALID / DISABLED states.
            '''

      stage: V2
      tests: ["keymgr_dpe_debug_reg"]
    }
    {
      name: err_code_reg
      desc: '''
            Perform operations that can trigger fields in ERR_CODE register.

            Stimulus:
            The list of stimuli includes:
            - Send an invalid operation. Examples:
              - "invalid_parent_policy_operations" test.
              - "invalid_slot_operations" test.
              - requesting a versioned key generation from a root key slot.
              - Issue an operation with CONTROL_SHADOWED.sw_binding_only=0 when
                ENFORCE_SW_BINDING.enforce is set.
            - Send an invalid KMAC input. One of the example can be "invalid_hw_bindings" test.
            - After an operation, overwrite a *shadowed register and expect to see
              ERR_CODE.invalid_shadow_update.
            - Issue operations when WORKING_STATE.state inside {RESET, DISABLED, INVALID}.

            Checks:
            - Expect to see the corresponding field in the ERR_CODE register be set.
            '''

      stage: V2
      tests: ["keymgr_dpe_err_code_reg"]
    }
    {
      name: fault_status_reg
      desc: '''
            Perform operations in a way that will trigger synchronous and asynchronous fatal faults.

            Stimulus:
            Examples of stimuli to trigger fatal faults are:
            - Drive invalid data to KMAC, sideload control, reseed_counter and control FSM.
            - Drive a register integrity and shadow copy errors.
            - Drive the reseed counter to a bad value.
            - Inject a METADATA ECC error.
            - Drive a non-onehot command to KMAC interface.
            - Drive bad response from KMAC.

            Checks:
            - Check that the alert is triggered.
            - Check both ERR_CODE and FAULT_STATUS bits to verify the register fields wiring with
              errors.
            '''

      stage: V2
      tests: ["keymgr_dpe_fault_status"]
    }
  ]
  covergroups: [
    {
      name: operation_cg
      desc: '''
            - Cover all operations crossed with all slots picked as source and destination, all
              boot stages included, and all working states.
            - Generate versioned key for all the HW sideload ports crossed with
              CONTROL_SHADOWED.dest_sel.
            - Sample with both DONE_SUCCESS and DONE_ERROR statuses in OP_STATUS.status.
            - Sample once each operation completes.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: lc_disable_cg
      desc: '''
             - Cover LC disable events during each operation type.
             - Sample when LC disables keymgr_dpe.
             '''
    }
    {
      name: sideload_clear_cg
      desc: '''
            - Cover all possible values for SIDELOAD_CLEAR.val used after versioned key generation
              for HW.
            - Cross it with the list of enumerated values outside the allowed list to see if all the
              sideload ports are continuously cleared.
            - Cross with every combination of sideload interface (AES, HMAC, KMAC, OTBN).
            - Cross it with both WORKING_STATE.state inside {AVAILABLE, DISABLED} (In DISABLED
              state, the SW and sideload keys are preserved).
            - Sample after Generate HW Output operation for HW.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: reseed_interval_cg
      desc: '''
            - Cover small PRNG intervals in RESEED_INTERVAL that the testbench can verify that EDN
              requests are issued frequently.
            - Also cover large values to ensure all register bits are toggled.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: wipe_keys_cg
      desc: '''
            - Covers that the SW keys in SW_SHARE*_OUTPUT registers and sideload ports are wiped
              only in invalid state.
            - Cross it with WORKING_STATE.state inside {DISABLED, INVALID}.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: wipe_slots_only_cg
      desc: '''
            - Covers that the slots are wiped and SW keys in SW_SHARE*_OUTPUT registers and sideload
              ports are preserved only in DISABLED state.
            - Cross it with WORKING_STATE.state inside {DISABLED, INVALID}.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: hw_sw_invalid_bindings_cg
      desc: '''
            - Cover toggle of all bits across HW / SW input registers:
              - SW_BINDING
              - SALT
              - KEY_VERSION
              - MAX_KEY_VERSION
              - All ones/zeros on the OTP root key.
              - All ones/zeros on ROM digest.
              - All ones/zeros on creator seed.
              - All ones/zeros on owner seed.
            - Cross with the corresponding regwen value, confirm writes are attempted
              while the regwen allows the write (unlocked) and while it blocks it (locked).
            '''
    }
    {
      name: enforce_sw_bindings_cg
      desc: '''
            - Covers an advance operation that uses SW binding only as input messages for KMAC key
              derivation function.
            - Cross with advance operation, CONTROL_SHADOWED.sw_binding_only and all boot stages.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: non_existing_slot_cg
      desc: '''
            Cover a scenario:
              CONTROL_SHADOWED.slot_src_sel / CONTROL_SHADOWED.slot_dst_sel > NumInstHwSlot.
            Cross with each operation.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: key_version_compare_cg
      desc: '''
            - Cover all comparison outcomes (equal, less than, greater than) between
              KEY_VERSION and the MAX_KEY_VER_SHADOWED registers.
            - Crossed with operation type GEN_SW_OUTPUT and GEN_HW_OUTPUT.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: kmac_err_rsp_cg
      desc: '''
            Cover a scenario where KMAC responds with an error that will trigger *kmac* fields in
            ERR_CODE and FAULT_STATUS registers.
            Cross it with advance, gen_sw_output and gen_hw_output commands.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: load_key_with_lock_cg
      desc: '''
            Load root / secondary root key when their respective *LOCK.lock register field is set.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: retain_parent_cg
      desc: '''
            - Cover SLOT_POLICY.retain_parent set / unset.
            - Cross it with CONTROL_SHADOWED.SLOT_SRC_SEL == CONTROL_SHADWED.SLOT_DST_SEL when
              retain_parent field is set and vice versa
            - Cross it with METADATA_HIGH.valid = false.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: allow_child_cg
      desc: '''
            - Cover SLOT_POLICY.allow_child = false, with a valid source slot, and an Advance
              operation issued.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: allow_keygen_cg
      desc: '''
            - Cover SLOT_POLICY.allow_keygen = false, with a valid source slot, and gen_sw_out or
              gen_hw_out operations issued.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: invalid_sw_input_cg
      desc: '''
            Write SW registers such as CONTROL_SHADOWED, MAX_KEY_VER_SHADOWED, KEY_VERSION, and
            SLOT_POLICY with invalid contents. Example:
            - Generating version key when KEY_VERSION > MAX_KEY_VERSION.
              CONTROL_SHADOWED.slot_src_sel / CONTROL_SHADOWED.slot_dst_sel > NumInstHwSlot.
            - Send a bad command.
            - Set bad content to SLOT_POLICY fields.
            - Write CONTROL_SHADOWED.dest_sel with undefined enumerated value.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: invalid_slot_op_cg
      desc: '''
            - Cover scenarios where an operations use invalid parent slot or valid child slot.
            - Cross it with every operation.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: invalid_kmac_command_cg
      desc: '''
            On KMAC interface, the commands issued by keymgr_dpe are onehot encoded. Send a
            non-onehot command to KMAC
            '''
    }
    {
      name: invalid_root_key_cg
      desc: '''
            - Cover the root keys (both root and secondary root) with either containing all-zeros /
              all-ones or METADATA_HIGH.valid field is low.
            - Cross it with WORKING_STATE.state inside {RESET, AVAILABLE} and operations inside
              {ADVANCE, LOAD_ROOT_KEY, LOAD_ROOT_SECONDARY_KEY}
            '''
    }
    {
      name: err_code_cg
      desc: '''
            - Cover all ERR_CODE register fields.
            - Sampled when ERR_CODE register is read.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: fault_status_cg
      desc: '''
            - Cover all FAULT_STATUS register fields.
            - Sampled when FAULT_STATUS is read.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: debug_cg
      desc: '''
            - Cover all DEBUG register fields.
            - Sampled when DEBUG is read.
            '''
    }
  ]
}
